 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery** ([docs/decisions/change-delivery.md](decisions/change-delivery.md)). Committed revisions are pushed to subscribed browsers over self-hosted WebSocket sessions, backed by replayable revisions and reliable publication in the change store and reconnect catch-up in the browser. Five-second HTTPS polling would meet the 15-second freshness goal with simpler stateless operation, but was rejected because it adds repeated read load and multi-second delivery lag; a hosted realtime service would offload connection management, but was rejected to avoid recurring vendor fees and provider coupling. MarketBoard accepts owning connection capacity and reconnect operations to deliver fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor.
